<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · lake</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kwan Phayao</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Phayao Lake</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Kwan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phayao">Phayao</a> is a large freshwater lake beside the town of Phayao in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thailand">Thailand</a>. It is the largest freshwater lake of the north and an important fishery. The old city of Phayao, capital of a medieval Tai kingdom, stood on its shore, and the ruins of a temple, Wat Tilok Aram, lie submerged in the lake.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kwan-phayao.jpg" alt="Kwan Phayao" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Chainwit. · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sunset_at_Kwan_Phayao_%E0%B8%81%E0%B8%A7%E0%B9%8A%E0%B8%B2%E0%B8%99%E0%B8%9E%E0%B8%B0%E0%B9%80%E0%B8%A2%E0%B8%B2_(25-12-2021)_img_05.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Kwan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phayao">Phayao</a> lies in Phayao Province in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thailand">Thailand</a>; kwan is a word of the northern Thai language meaning a lake or large swamp. The lake has the shape of a half moon, a depth of about 1.5 metres and an area of about 1,980 hectares, the fourth largest in the country. It lies in a basin, once surrounded by low mountains, formed by a collapse of the lithosphere about 70 million years ago, where 18 creeks flow together. The land around it is very fertile, and communities have lived there since ancient times.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Before 1941 the area was a broad lowland, wet in the rainy season from about July to November. The Ing River flowed through it and formed many large ponds called nong and smaller ones called buak, and in flood these joined into two lakes, Kwan Noi in the west and Kwan Luang in the east. In the dry season farmers used the land for crops and animals and as a route from the town to the surrounding villages, while fishing there was crowded and uncontrolled.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">Department</a> of Fisheries began a floodgate in 1939 and finished it in 1941. The rising water flooded fields, houses, a temple and an archaeological site in what had been an urban area with many temples, and the natural swamps became large <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reservoir">reservoirs</a>. The department set up a fisheries research station to breed fish for the lake, part of a campaign to provide a cheap protein diet.</p><p>By 2009 the lake had become shallow and degraded, clogged with weeds and sediment, and its fish stocks had dwindled. The Hydro and Agro Informatics Institute and other agencies then built dikes to trap sediment, removed the weeds for animal feed and restocked the lake with native fish. Its storage grew by 370,000 cubic metres, enough to supply 16,500 nearby families and 43,785 rai of farmland.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>About 50 species of fish live in the lake, among them the iridescent shark, common carp, climbing perch, striped snakehead and great white sheatfish, and the harvest is estimated at about 159 kilograms per hectare. Of the 36 recorded kinds of aquatic plants, water hyacinth covers about a third of the lake.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Phayao Lake” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Northern Thai chronicles say Phayao was founded in the late 11th century by Khun Chom Tham, a prince of the Ngoenyang line, on the shores of the lake now called <b>Kwan Phayao</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/phayao-kingdom">Phayao Kingdom</a></li></ul></section>
